"id":33523684344016,"type":"artist","name":"Nick McCarthy","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":4,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":44500101503623,"type":"artist","name":"Nick McCarthy (guitarist, bass player)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"member_of":[],"genres":[{"id":53692558339894,"type":"genre","name":"experimental","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":63,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":247763400229876,"type":"genre","name":"Crout Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":7,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":226407294048477,"type":"genre","name":"Jazzy","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":2,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":213657441221020,"type":"genre","name":"Prog Rock-ish","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_genres":[{"id":4451667526661,"type":"genre","name":"Krautrock","n":9},{"id":117363531556446,"type":"genre","name":"Jazz-Rock","n":6},{"id":141489031108271,"type":"genre","name":"Arabic Jazz","n":2},{"id":55893123468232,"type":"genre","name":"Experimental Rock","n":2},{"id":41931795354963,"type":"genre","name":"Afro-Jazz","n":2},{"id":265524373450282,"type":"genre","name":"jazz","n":1},{"id":249867398295157,"type":"genre","name":"Fon Music","n":1},{"id":227869900047075,"type":"genre","name":"Soca","n":1},{"id":191826593279813,"type":"genre","name":"Art Rock","n":1},{"id":190589917177789,"type":"genre","name":"Jam Band","n":1},{"id":160717495480047,"type":"genre","name":"Yoruba Music","n":1},{"id":147423806094006,"type":"genre","name":"Psychedelic Rock","n":1},{"id":117881556484276,"type":"genre","name":"Arabic Music","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":107234531659708,"name":"Kosmische Musik y krautrock","book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","start":1968,"end":1978,"desc":"El rock británico recicla el americano; el alemán inventa otro tipo de rock (a menudo, ni siquiera rock). Los músicos alemanes ven la relación entre la psicodelia y la vanguardia electrónica de Stockhausen: en 1968 nace el Zodiak Free Arts Lab de Berlín, en 1969 debutan Can, en 1971 Tangerine Dream inventan la «kosmische musik» con sintetizadores y secuenciadores y el viaje del acid-rock se convierte en viaje por el cosmos; en 1972 la larga suite electrónica (Zeit, Irrlicht) fija el formato que se copiará treinta años. Amon Duul II (gótico teutón), Can (el estudio del ritmo y la textura: Tago Mago, Future Days), Holger Czukay (world music electrónica), Tangerine Dream, Klaus Schulze, Ash Ra Tempel, Guru Guru, Agitation Free y otros. Ningún movimiento, salvo los primeros rockeros, influyó en tantos músicos durante tantas décadas.","color":"#38bdf8","family":"Progresivo, krautrock y jazz-rock","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#kosmische"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":231516142554711,"artist":"Embryo","artist_id":213223401577391,"title":"Opal","year":1970,"ess":1,"yt":"H-Z_SG6FmaI","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Kosmische Musik y krautrock","scene_id":107234531659708,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#kosmische","chapters":["Sección 3.7"],"pages":[[1,70,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":70,"text":"…(1974). More derivative progressive-rock was played by bands such as: Xhol, whose Electrip (1969) parallels Frank Zappa's developments in jazz-rock; Embryo (1), whose Opal (1970) was influenced by John Coltrane's mystic free-jazz; Organisation (later evolved into Kraftwerk), whose Tone Float (1970) was…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Formation and Longevity","body":"Embryo is a German band that formed in 1969 and continues to exist today. \"This was a German band, which was formed in 1969 and still exists today.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Franz Ferdinand: In Their Own Words","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RWbKqqW78yg"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Nick McCarthy's Involvement","body":"Nick McCarthy was \"in and out\" of Embryo, joining them while growing up in Munich. He toured the world as their bass player before eventually moving back to Glasgow in 2001. \"guitarist Nick McCarthy was in and out of Embryo. He joined them while he was growing up in Munich and toured the world as their bass player.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Franz Ferdinand: In Their Own Words","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RWbKqqW78yg"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[{"kind":"xref","title":"In Utero","body":null,"year":null,"extra":{"from":"Kurt Cobain","from_id":93654464894046,"type":"album"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Stupid History: The Music Version 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7kH_AfT3i4k"}]},{"kind":"xref","title":"Medical Fetish – Anatomy Art, Gynecological Instruments, Full-Sized Medical Dummy on Tour","body":"Kurt Cobain's artistic aesthetic included a deep preoccupation with medical imagery and human anatomy that dated back to his grade school drawings. The artwork for *In Utero* features disassembled dolls, fetuses, and embryo-like forms. He reportedly collected vintage medical equipment and surgical instruments, including old gynecological devices well beyond basic medical tools. One account claims he kept a full-sized medical dummy at home and took it on tour in its own trunk. His journals (published by Courtney Love in 2002) reveal an obsession with bodily functions, vomit, urine, mucus, and excrement — consistent with his naming of his first band Fecal Matter. \"Kurt had a full-sized medical dummy. Not only did he keep it around the house, but he toured with it.\"","year":null,"extra":{"from":"Nirvana","from_id":119070739316349,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"100 Weirdest Things in Alt-Rock: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e6S5CTX7kkE"}]},{"kind":"xref","title":"Mentioned as Pre-Franz Ferdinand Band","body":"10P Invaders is listed as one of the obscure bands that future Franz Ferdinand members played in during the 1990s in Glasgow, though no specific musical details or releases are provided in the transcript. \"unless you were going to gigs at places like King Tutzer Wabahutzer or Sloan's or the Cathouse or the old fruit market, you've never heard of bands like Corelia or Yummie Fur or Embryo or 10P Invaders.\"","year":null,"extra":{"from":"10P Invaders","from_id":73205387202304,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Franz Ferdinand: In Their Own Words","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RWbKqqW78yg"}]},{"kind":"xref","title":"Nick McCarthy's Post-Embryo Band","body":"After his time with Embryo, Nick McCarthy joined this German jazz band as a bass player before moving back to Glasgow in 2001. \"He eventually joined a German jazz band called Scatter on Bass before he decided to move back to Glasgow in 2001.\"","year":null,"extra":{"from":"Scatter","from_id":264486353544912,"type":"curiosity"},"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Franz Ferdinand: In Their Own Words","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RWbKqqW78yg"}]}],"charts":[{"id":231516142554711,"type":"album","name":"Opal","list_id":23428794615065,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Krautrock","src":"musthear","rank":151,"year":1970,"note":null,"yt":"H-Z_SG6FmaI","scaruffi":7.0},{"id":231516142554711,"type":"album","name":"Opal","work_year":1970,"list_id":65232636722211,"list":"Scaruffi 1970s","src":"charts","rank":271,"year":1970,"note":null,"yt":"H-Z_SG6FmaI","scaruffi":7.0},{"id":162883674544541,"type":"album","name":"Embryo's Rache","list_id":145636818792428,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Jazz-Rock","src":"musthear","rank":144,"year":1971,"note":null,"yt":"lMlp4eCabUo","scar